
Experiment 5:Develop a program to deploy smart street light system using LDR sensor
Algorithm
1.	Start. 
2.	Initialize the LDR and LED pins. 
3.	Configure the LED pin as OUTPUT. 
4.	Read the light intensity from the LDR sensor. 
5.	Compare the LDR value with the threshold (500). 
6.	If the light intensity is below the threshold, turn the LED ON. 
7.	Otherwise, turn the LED OFF. 
8.	Repeat the process continuously. 
Line-by-Line Program Explanation
const int ldrPin = A0;          // Connect LDR output to Analog Pin A0.

const int ledPin = 8;           // LED connected to Digital Pin 8.

int ldrValue;                   // Variable to store LDR value.

pinMode(ledPin, OUTPUT);        // Configure LED pin as OUTPUT.

Serial.begin(9600);             // Start Serial Monitor.

ldrValue = analogRead(ldrPin);  // Read light intensity.

Serial.println(ldrValue);       // Display LDR value on Serial Monitor.

if(ldrValue < 500)              // Check if it is dark.
{
    digitalWrite(ledPin, HIGH); // Turn ON street light.
}
else
{
    digitalWrite(ledPin, LOW);  // Turn OFF street light.
}

delay(200);                     // Wait for 200 milliseconds.


Working
•	The LDR sensor continuously measures the ambient light intensity. 
•	During daylight, the LDR value is high, so the LED (street light) remains OFF. 
•	During darkness or low light, the LDR value falls below the threshold (500), causing the Arduino to turn the LED ON. 
•	The process repeats continuously, providing automatic street light control. 
